# launch.json and tasks.json reference your integration domain
# in debugger arguments and task commands.
.vscode/launch.json
.vscode/tasks.json

# ── User-specific project files ──────────────────────────────
# These are customized for your integration during initialization
README.md
README.template.md
LICENSE
initialize.sh
hacs.json
CONTRIBUTING.md

# Deleted here — the blueprint's DEPENDENCIES.md only restates what
# requirements*.txt, package.json and manifest.json already say. Listed so sync
# does not re-add it.
DEPENDENCIES.md

# ── AI agent instruction files ───────────────────────────────
# Project-identity files are customized during initialization and must not be
# overwritten by template sync (they contain your domain, class prefix, repo
# URL, etc.). The path-specific *.instructions.md files under .agents/instructions/
# use generic placeholders and ARE safely synced from the template.
AGENTS.md
CODEX.md
CLAUDE.md
GEMINI.md

# GitHub Copilot is not used on this project: AGENTS.md serves the agents we do
# use. Both paths are deleted here, so they are ignored to keep sync from
# re-adding them.
.github/copilot-instructions.md
.github/prompts/

# The blueprint ships .github/instructions as a symlink to .agents/instructions
# so Copilot and VS Code discover the files at their conventional path. This
# project reads .agents/instructions/ directly, so the symlink is deleted here
# and ignored to keep sync from re-adding it. It also cannot be synced cleanly:
# sync cannot replace a directory with a symlink and drops it next to the old
# one under a mangled `instructions~<sha>` name instead (#77).
.github/instructions
.github/instructions~*

# Claude Code is the only agent used here. Its own configuration is owned
# below; every other agent's is ignored so a sync cannot reintroduce it.
.codex/
.gemini/
.cursor/

# ── DevContainer AI tooling ──────────────────────────────────
# Which coding agents this DevContainer provisions, and how permissively, is a
# maintainer decision rather than blueprint tooling. #64 would have added a
# Copilot CLI wrapper defaulting to --allow-all-tools, a Codex CLI config
# setting danger-full-access, Claude Code managed settings, persistent agent
# home volumes and a `copilot` shell alias — the same reasoning that already
# keeps .github/copilot-instructions.md and .github/prompts/ out, above.
# Note that devcontainer.json and .vscode/extensions.json stay synced because
# they carry mostly unrelated settings, so agent-specific entries arriving in
# those two still need review on each sync. #77 arrived with a Codex
# devcontainer feature, a persistent /home/vscode/.codex volume and an
# openai.chatgpt extension recommendation; all three were dropped, and the
# Claude Code equivalents were kept. Expect the same three back on every sync.
# Trade-off: unrelated on-create.sh and shell startup improvements now have to
# be copied over by hand — `script/compare-blueprint` shows what changed.
.devcontainer/claude-code/
.devcontainer/codex/
.devcontainer/copilot/
.devcontainer/on-create.sh
.devcontainer/.bashrc
.devcontainer/.zshrc

# The four instruction files for features this integration does not have —
# services.yaml, repairs.py, service_actions/, configuration.yaml — used to be
# excluded here. They are synced again since #77: the blueprint's skill graph
# links them (ha-service-action/SKILL.md, blueprint.config_flow), so excluding
# them turns script/skills-check red on dangling pointers. An instruction file
# only loads when a matching file is edited, and no file here matches any of
# the four, so shipping them unused costs nothing.

# Schema for the services.yaml this integration does not ship.
schemas/yaml/services_schema.yaml

